.TH scat "" "Command"
.SH NAME
scat \- segmented concatenation
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBscat [ [\fIoption ...\fB] [\fIfile ...\fB] ] ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
scat -- segmented concatenation
Usage:	scat [ [ options ] [ file ... ] ] ...
Options:
	-bn	Begin output at line n
	-c	Mark control characters (overrides -t)
	-in	Skip n columns on output
	-ln	Set screen length to n lines
	-n	Number input lines
	-r	Remote--no paging
	-s	Squash empty lines
	-Sn	Seek n bytes into input before processing
	-t	Truncate lines to line length (default: wraparound)
	-wn	Set screen width to n columns
	-x	Expand tabs
Responses to pause for pagination:
	RETURN	Next page
	SPACE	Next line
	/	Next half page
	f	Print file name and line number
	n	Next file
	q	Quit
.HE
.B scat
copies each named
.I file
to standard output, one screen-page at a time.
With no
.I file
arguments,
.B scat
reads standard input.
When output is a terminal,
.B scat
pauses after each page and reads a single character to decide what to
do next; when output is not a terminal, or when
.B \-r
is given, it runs straight through without pausing.
.PP
.B scat
scans two argument lists: first the environment variable
.B SCAT
(a string of options separated by blanks, tabs, or newlines, with no
quoting), then the command-line arguments.
Options may be interspersed with file names and take effect for the
files that follow.
An argument of the form
.BI \-. suffix
followed by more options associates those options only with a later
file whose name ends in
.BI . suffix ;
the sublist of options it introduces ends at the next file name, at
.BR \-\- ,
or at another
.BR \-. \fIsuffix\fR .
An argument of
.B \-\-
ends a sublist, or (outside a sublist) ends option scanning so that
later arguments are taken as file names even if they begin with `\-'.
.PP
Numeric option arguments may begin with
.B 0
for octal, and may be followed by
.B b
or
.B k
to be scaled by 512 or 1024.
.PP
Options:
.IP \fB\-b\fIn\fR
Discard the first
.I n
lines of the file before displaying anything.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Display control characters unambiguously: a character in the range
0-037 is shown as a caret followed by the character 0100 higher (so
SOH appears as
.BR ^A ),
and a character with the top bit set is shown with a leading
.BR ~ .
The characters
.BR ^ ", " ~ ", and " \e
are themselves prefixed with
.BR \e .
This overrides
.BR \-t .
Two sub-options may follow directly after
.BR \-c :
.B s
also maps blanks to
.B _
(prefixing a literal underscore with
.BR \e ),
and
.B t
prints tabs literally instead of as
.BR ^I .
.IP \fB\-i\fIn\fR
Shift the display window
.I n
columns to the right, discarding that many leading columns of each
line; useful for viewing long lines.
.IP \fB\-l\fIn\fR
Use
.I n
as the screen length in lines, instead of the default of 24 (34 for a
Tektronix 4012 terminal).
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Prefix each (non-wrapped) input line with its line number.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Remote mode: write straight through without pausing for pagination,
even if standard output is a terminal.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Squash empty lines out of the output.
.IP \fB\-S\fIn\fR
Seek
.I n
bytes into the file before starting to read it.
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Truncate lines that are wider than the screen, instead of the default
of wrapping the excess onto the next line (marked with a trailing
.BR \e ).
.IP \fB\-w\fIn\fR
Use
.I n
as the screen width in columns, instead of the default of 80 (72 for
a Tektronix 4012 terminal).
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Expand tabs to spaces on output.
.PP
While paused between pages,
.B scat
accepts a single character in response:
.RS
.IP \fBRETURN\fR
Show the next page.
.IP \fBSPACE\fR
Show one more line.
.IP \fB/\fR
Show the next half page.
.IP \fBf\fR
Print the current file name and line number, then continue waiting.
.IP \fBn\fR
Skip to the next file.
.IP \fBq\fR
Quit
.B scat
immediately.
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B cat
.SH NOTES
.B scat
recognizes the terminal type
.B 4012
(from the
.B TERM
environment variable) specially, choosing a different default page
length and width and a different erase sequence for that terminal.
